From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-agent 976: report the uplink we actually have, not the first one we ever had

handle_topology_discovery() keeps a->ul_dev current on every discovery, but wrote
it to /var/run/multiap/multiap.backhaul only when the keys were absent. The first
parent a node ever associated with was therefore the last one it ever reported.
Reassociating to a different upstream left the file untouched for the rest of the
node's uptime.

That file is not internal state. It is what ubus backhaul_info answers from, and
through that answer it is where the controller's idea of who is whose parent comes
from. A node that has moved keeps telling everyone it has not.

Measured on hardware 2026-07-31. The node had moved to the other agent and knew it
- iw reported the new AP MLD and both sides agreed on the 1905 link - while the
file still named the controller from the previous association:

  /var/run/multiap/multiap.backhaul
    backhaul_device_id  86:f7:56:41:01:d3     (controller)
    backhaul_macaddr    86:f7:56:41:01:d4
  iw dev bsta-mld-3 link
    Connected to        e6:a9:d2:c1:98:40     (the other agent)

The controller consequently recorded the wrong parent and a depth one hop short,
and querying could not correct it: every answer repeated the stale value. This is
the mechanism behind "agent reports the wrong parent", open since 2026-07-29 with
the symptom described but no cause.

The condition now also fires when the stored value differs from the current one.
Addresses are compared as addresses, not as text, so formatting or case cannot be
mistaken for a change of parent; an unparseable stored value counts as stale,
which is the safe direction - at worst the file is rewritten with what we already
know to be true.

Note for anyone editing this: the policy order is not the call order. tb[1] is
backhaul_macaddr and tb[2] is backhaul_device_id, while the helper takes the
device id first.

The write itself is unchanged and still goes through multiap
set_uplink_backhaul_info, which holds map.backhaul.lock, so this adds no new
writer and no new race.

What this does not address: the file is written by the agent process while
genconfig writes the same tree from two other places. Serialising those is 977;
this patch only stops the agent from writing something it knows to be false.

Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>

--- a/src/agent_map.c	2026-07-31 19:36:51.941003165 +0200
+++ b/src/agent_map.c	2026-07-31 19:36:51.941240653 +0200
@@ -739,10 +739,58 @@
 	strncpy(ul_ifname, blobmsg_data(tb[0]), IFNAMSIZ - 1);
 
 	if (!strncmp(ul_ifname, cmdu->dev_ifname, IFNAMSIZ)) {
+		uint8_t file_almac[6] = {0};
+		uint8_t file_hwaddr[6] = {0};
+		int stale;
+
 		memcpy(a->ul_dev.ul_almac, almac, 6);
 		memcpy(a->ul_dev.ul_hwaddr, hwaddr, 6);
 
-		if (!tb[1] || !tb[2]) {
+		/* Write it out whenever it differs, not only when it is absent.
+		 *
+		 * The two memcpy above keep our own idea of the uplink current, but
+		 * the file is what everyone else reads - ubus backhaul_info, and
+		 * through it the controller's picture of who is whose parent. The
+		 * old condition wrote it only when the keys were missing, so the
+		 * first parent a node ever had was the last one it ever reported.
+		 * Reassociating to a different upstream never updated it.
+		 *
+		 * Measured on hardware 2026-07-31. Node had moved to the other
+		 * agent and knew it - iw showed the new AP MLD, and both sides
+		 * agreed on the 1905 link - while the file still named the
+		 * controller from the previous association:
+		 *
+		 *   /var/run/multiap/multiap.backhaul
+		 *     backhaul_device_id  86:f7:56:41:01:d3   (controller)
+		 *     backhaul_macaddr    86:f7:56:41:01:d4
+		 *   iw dev bsta-mld-3 link
+		 *     Connected to        e6:a9:d2:c1:98:40   (the other agent)
+		 *
+		 * The controller therefore recorded the wrong parent and a depth
+		 * one hop short, and no amount of querying could correct it,
+		 * because the agent kept answering with the stale value.
+		 *
+		 * Note the policy order: tb[1] is backhaul_macaddr and tb[2] is
+		 * backhaul_device_id, which is the reverse of the order they are
+		 * passed to the helper below.
+		 *
+		 * The stored values are compared as addresses rather than as text,
+		 * so a difference in formatting or case cannot be mistaken for a
+		 * change of parent - and an unparseable value counts as stale,
+		 * which is the safe direction: at worst the file is rewritten with
+		 * what we already know to be true.
+		 */
+		stale = !tb[1] || !tb[2] ||
+			!hwaddr_aton(blobmsg_data(tb[2]), file_almac) ||
+			!hwaddr_aton(blobmsg_data(tb[1]), file_hwaddr) ||
+			memcmp(file_almac, a->ul_dev.ul_almac, 6) ||
+			memcmp(file_hwaddr, a->ul_dev.ul_hwaddr, 6);
+
+		if (stale) {
+			dbg("%s: uplink now " MACFMT " via " MACFMT ", updating %s\n",
+			    __func__, MAC2STR(a->ul_dev.ul_almac),
+			    MAC2STR(a->ul_dev.ul_hwaddr), MAP_UPLINK_PATH);
+
 			runCmd("/lib/wifi/multiap set_uplink_backhaul_info "
 					MACFMT " " MACFMT,
 					MAC2STR(a->ul_dev.ul_almac),
